      "tags": ["Typography", "Leading", "Readability"],
      "body": "<h2>The invisible architecture of text</h2><p>Open any design textbook and you\u2019ll find chapters on typeface selection, font pairing, and hierarchy. Leading \u2014 the space between lines \u2014 rarely gets more than a paragraph. Yet leading is the invisible grid that either lets text breathe or chokes it to death.</p><blockquote><p>Good typography is invisible. The reader doesn\u2019t notice it \u2014 they just find the text easy, even pleasurable, to read.</p></blockquote><h2>What the masters knew</h2><p>Jan Tschichold understood that line height wasn\u2019t decoration \u2014 it was structure. He prescribed leading as a function of typeface design, measure (line length), and reading distance.</p><ul><li>Body text typically needs 120\u2013145% leading</li><li>Display type can go tighter \u2014 even 90% in all-caps settings</li><li>Small text (under 12px) often needs 150% or more</li></ul><h2>Digital degrades leading</h2><p>CSS\u2019s <code>line-height: 1.5</code> has become the default across the web \u2014 a compromise that works reasonably for nothing and brilliantly for nothing. The magic number doesn\u2019t exist. Leading is a design decision.</p>"
