      "tags": ["Typography", "Hierarchy", "Weight"],
      "body": "<h2>The tyranny of scale</h2><p>When students first learn typographic hierarchy, they reach for the most obvious tool: size. But professional typographers know that hierarchy can be established through weight, spacing, and position alone, with far more nuance.</p><blockquote><p>Restraint is not weakness. It is the hardest skill to develop and the clearest signal of mastery.</p></blockquote><h2>Weight as signal</h2><p>A semibold heading at the same size as body text, set above a hairline rule, communicates hierarchy without shouting. The relationship is established through contrast of weight, not scale.</p>"
